 ## Subtopics
 
 ### WKB-based Multiple Scattering Approximation
-An approximation for multiple scattering in participating media that replaces integration over complete $(x_0, \tilde{\theta}_0)$-space with integration over a reduced subset selected by a set of Multiple Path Points (MPPs). It relies on the assumption that single scattered light distribution is strongly forward peaked [S1]. It is chosen when multiple scattering effects are significant but the forward-peaking assumption holds, allowing for efficient integration over a reduced path space.
+An approximation for multiple scattering in participating media that replaces integration over complete $(x_0, \tilde{\theta}_0)$-space with integration over a reduced subset selected by a set of Multiple Path Points (MPPs) [S1]. It relies on the assumption that single scattered light distribution is strongly forward peaked [S1]. It is chosen when multiple scattering effects are significant but the forward-peaking assumption holds, allowing for efficient integration over a reduced path space [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the cost of finding the MPP and evaluating the Gaussian integral. Generally faster than full path integral methods for highly scattering media where the MPP dominates.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, as it only stores the MPP and local curvature information.
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles multiple scattering by focusing on the most probable paths. Provides a physical interpretation of radiance blurring.
-*   **Limitations:** Accuracy degrades if the action landscape is not well-approximated by a quadratic form (i.e., if the MPP is not a sharp minimum). Relies on the forward-peaking assumption.
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for participating media with strong multiple scattering and forward-peaked phase functions.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Unsuitable for media with isotropic scattering or when the MPP is not well-defined.
